“The GIL, or Global Interpreter Lock…
is a feature in Python…
that allows only one thread to execute at a time…
even if multiple threads are created.
In simple terms…
Python doesn’t run threads in true parallel…
because the GIL allows only one thread to use the CPU at a time.
The main reason for GIL is…
to make memory management safe and simple…
and to avoid issues like race conditions.
So because of this…
GIL becomes a limitation for CPU-heavy tasks…
like data processing…
since threads can’t run in parallel.
But for I/O tasks…
like API calls or file operations…
GIL is not a big problem…
because threads spend time waiting.
To overcome this…
we use multiprocessing…
where multiple processes run independently…
and use multiple CPU cores.
In short…
GIL helps with safety…
but limits parallel execution in Python.”
